course: everyday
lesson: 117
title: Neighbours, deliveries, heating and cooling
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Neighbours are the people closest to your home, and good neighbours help each other, respect each other's property and look out for each other.
explain: Respect means small things: asking before picking someone's flowers, knocking to ask for a lost ball instead of climbing their fence, and playing where your ball will not hit their car.
explain: Looking out for each other means noticing too: if an elderly neighbour who lives alone has not been seen for a while, telling a grown-up can bring help to someone who has fallen.
explain: For example, a girl in London kicks her ball over the fence; she knocks on the door with her dad and asks politely, and the neighbour is happy to hand it back.
explain: The home's heating and cooling need care as well: heaters burn skin and can set fabric alight, and on hot days you keep the sun out in the day and let cool air in at night.
fact: When a new family moves in next door, saying hello with a grown-up is a friendly start.
fact: To get a ball back from a neighbour's garden, knock and ask politely rather than climbing the fence.
fact: An elderly neighbour who lives alone may fall and need help, so tell a grown-up if you have not seen them for a while.
fact: A dog behind a fence may bite, so it should not be teased and hands should not go through the fence.
fact: A child checks with a parent before going inside a neighbour's house alone, so a grown-up knows where they are.
fact: Picking flowers from someone's garden needs their permission first.
fact: A parcel for a neighbour is taken in only if a parent agrees, and given back the same day.
fact: Some door-to-door sellers are scams, so say no politely and close the door or fetch a grown-up.
fact: Radiators and heaters can burn skin, so you do not lean or sit on them.
fact: A portable heater must be kept away from curtains and bedding and never covered, or it can start a fire.
fact: To keep a house cool in hot weather, close the curtains in the day, open the windows at night and drink water.
exercise: A new family moves in next door. What is a good thing to do? | Say hello and introduce yourself with a grown-up | Watch them through the window for a week and say nothing at all | Ask what they have in their boxes | Wait a year before saying anything
exercise: Your ball goes over the fence into the neighbour's garden. What do you do? | Knock and ask politely to get it back | Climb over the fence quickly to get it | Wait until dark and then sneak in for it | Throw another ball over to knock it back
exercise: Why should you not climb a neighbour's fence? | It is their property, and fences can break or hurt you | Fences are always freshly painted | It is quicker to walk round | Neighbours never give balls back, so there is no point asking
exercise: An old man next door lives alone and you have not seen him for days. What is kind and wise? | Tell a grown-up so they can check on him | Assume he has gone on holiday | Peep through his letterbox every day to see if he moves | Wait until he comes out by himself
exercise: The neighbour's dog barks at the fence. What should you do? | Leave it alone and keep your hands out of the fence | Bark back to tease it | Poke a stick through the gaps so it has something to play with | Reach through to pat it
exercise: A neighbour invites you inside for juice while you are playing alone. What do you do? | Check with your parent first | Go in quickly before your parent sees | Say yes and stay there for hours | Climb in through the back window
exercise: Where should you kick a ball against a wall? | Somewhere it will not damage anyone's car, windows or wall | Against the neighbour's car | Against the neighbour's front window, where the wall is flattest | Against their garage door at night
exercise: You love the roses in the neighbour's garden. Can you pick one? | Only if you ask first and they say yes | Yes, flowers belong to everyone | Yes, if nobody is looking | Yes, if you only pick a small one from the back of the bed
exercise: A courier (a parcel delivery driver) asks you to take a parcel for the neighbour. What is right? | Take it only if a parent agrees, and give it back the same day | Take it and open it carefully to see what is inside, then wrap it up | Keep it until the neighbour asks for it | Leave it in the street for them
exercise: A man at the door is selling cleaning cloths and asks to come in. What do you do? | Politely say no and close the door, or get a grown-up | Let him in to show you more | Give him a few coins from your piggy bank so that he goes away | Tell him your parents are out
exercise: Why should you not sit on a hot radiator? | It can burn your skin | It makes the radiator colder | It breaks the boiler downstairs | It turns the heating off
exercise: Where should a portable heater be put? | Away from curtains and bedding, and never covered | Under your duvet to warm the bed | Behind the curtains to hide it | With a damp towel over it so that the towel dries quicker
exercise: Your house is too hot in summer. What helps? | Close curtains in the day, open windows at night and drink water | Open all the curtains wide in the midday sun to let the breeze through | Close every window at night | Turn the oven on to move the air
test: Ruby in Toronto sees the post piling up outside the door of the old lady next door. What should she do? | Tell her parents so someone can check on her | Take the post home to read it | Ignore it, because what happens next door is not her business | Push the post through the window
test: Which is the best way to get your frisbee back from a neighbour's roof garden? | Knock on their door and ask politely | Climb up the drainpipe | Throw stones to knock it down | Leave a rude note on their door telling them to throw it back
test: Why do grown-ups want to know before you go into a neighbour's house? | A grown-up should always know where you are | Neighbours' houses are always dirty | Parents want to visit too | It is against the law to visit anyone
test: A stranger at the door says he is selling a special prize to lucky families. What is most likely? | It may be a scam, so say no and fetch a grown-up | He has really picked your family out of the whole street to win | You should give him your parents' bank card | You should let him in to explain
test: Why is it a bad idea to dry socks on top of a portable heater? | Covering the heater can make it catch fire | The socks will shrink too much | The heater will get too cold | The socks will smell of smoke only
test: Why close the curtains on a hot sunny afternoon? | They keep the sun's heat out of the room | They make the air wetter | They make the sun go down sooner so evening comes early | They let more cool air in
test: Why is it polite to ask before picking a neighbour's flowers? | The garden and its flowers belong to someone else | Flowers die if they are not picked | Neighbours like being woken up | Asking makes flowers grow faster
test: Which is true about a dog barking behind its own fence? | It may bite a hand that comes through | It barks only because it wants a treat | Barking dogs never ever bite anyone at all | It wants you to climb over the fence and play
test: A delivery driver leaves a neighbour's parcel with your family. What is the right thing? | Give it to the neighbour the same day | Keep it until they come and ask | Open it to check that nothing inside has been broken | Send it back to the shop
test: ball went over next door's fence again, just hop over? | No, knock and ask politely | Yes, quickly before they see | Yes, fences are for climbing | Leave it there and buy a new one
test: guy at the door selling stuff, says its a bargain | Say no politely and close the door or get a grown-up | Let him in to show you | Give him money to be nice | Tell him where your parents keep their money so he can wait
test: freezing, can i sit on the radiator | No, it can burn you, sit near it | Yes, radiators never get really hot | Yes, if you sit on a thick cushion | Yes, if it's only turned down low
test: its sooo hot, open the windows all day yeah? | Close curtains in the day, open windows at night, drink water | Open everything at noon | Keep the oven door open all day to dry the air and make a breeze | Wear lots of layers to trap cool
explain: A family in Ohio ran a generator inside the garage during a power cut; the carbon monoxide made them all dizzy and sick, and they were saved when a neighbour called 911.
explain: A boy in Leeds put a towel over a heater to dry it; it started to burn and filled the room with smoke.
fact: Carbon monoxide has no colour or smell and can kill, which is why homes have carbon monoxide alarms.
fact: Engines and generators must never run indoors or in a garage, because they make carbon monoxide.
fact: If a carbon monoxide alarm goes off, or everyone gets headaches and feels dizzy, get everyone out into fresh air and call the emergency number.
fact: Clothes or towels on heaters can catch fire.
test: carbon monoxide alarm going off, everyone has headaches | Get everyone out into fresh air and call the emergency number | Open one window a little and stay in bed until the headache passes | Turn the alarm off and sleep | Wait to see if it stops
test: drying my socks on the heater | Don't cover the heater, it can catch fire | Fine if they're thin | Only at night | Cover it all to dry faster
test: parcel guy says he needs to come in to sign | Don't let him in, get your parent | Let him in quick | Open the back door instead | Go out to his van
test: did u hear a whole family nearly died cos they ran a generator in the garage, a neighbour saved em. real lesson? | Carbon monoxide kills with no smell, so never run engines inside | Generators are only dangerous if they are old | Garages are safe if the door is half open | You will always smell the gas in plenty of time to get out safely
test: kids reckon old Mr Hill next door "hasn't been seen in a year, he's a ghost now" lol. what's the real thing to do | Tell a grown-up you have not seen him, he may need help | Leave him alone, it is only a ghost story | Climb his fence and look through every window to find him yourself | Post it online so everyone can check on him
test: Ruby's towel on the heater set off the smoke alarm n the whole street says her house "nearly blew up". pass it on? | No, that is exaggerated; the lesson is never put cloth on heaters | Yes, people should keep well away from her house in case it blows up | Yes, but tell it as a funny story only | Yes, because the alarm really went off
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a portable heater must not be covered or placed near curtains and bedding. | It may set the fabric alight and start a fire | It will cease to produce any heat at all | It will make the curtains fade in colour | It will use a great deal more electricity and raise the bill
exam: Whereas a child's ball has passed into a neighbour's garden, state the conduct consistent with good neighbourly practice. | Knock on the door and request its return politely | Climb over the fence quickly to retrieve it before anyone sees | Abandon the ball and purchase another one | Wait until nightfall and recover it unseen
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the maintenance of a cool dwelling in hot weather. | Close curtains by day, open windows at night | Open curtains by day, shut windows at night | Keep all windows closed and the heater on | Open the fridge door to cool down the room
exam: It is given that a door-to-door seller calls at a residence. Determine the appropriate response of a child. | Say no politely, close the door or fetch an adult | Let the seller in to show the product to the family | Hand over pocket money to be polite to the seller | Give the seller the family's phone number
